1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,096
How do you follow one payment through the whole system?

2
00:00:03,146 --> 00:00:04,968
A merchant calls the bank.

3
00:00:05,018 --> 00:00:08,376
My customer's payment has been spinning for a minute, they say.

4
00:00:08,426 --> 00:00:15,216
That payment crossed the payment page, ecom, the card zone, an adapter and the processing centre.

5
00:00:15,266 --> 00:00:19,656
It touched four machines, and each machine writes its own log lines.

6
00:00:19,706 --> 00:00:23,760
Without one number to follow, someone searches four logs and guesses.

7
00:00:23,810 --> 00:00:28,032
Worse, the lines that would help most are the ones most likely to hold a card number.

8
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:29,904
Think of a hospital.

9
00:00:29,954 --> 00:00:32,976
When you arrive, you get a wristband with one number.

10
00:00:33,026 --> 00:00:38,856
That number is on every chart, in every department: the lab, the scan, the ward.

11
00:00:38,906 --> 00:00:42,024
Anyone can follow your whole stay by that one number.

12
00:00:42,074 --> 00:00:45,552
Here, every request gets the same kind of number.

13
00:00:45,602 --> 00:00:51,504
We call it a trace: one number that follows a request from the moment it arrives until its answer leaves.

14
00:00:51,554 --> 00:00:54,888
When ecom calls the card zone, the trace goes along.

15
00:00:54,938 --> 00:00:58,200
When the card zone calls an adapter, it goes along again.

16
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:01,968
So one request draws one line across both products.

17
00:01:02,018 --> 00:01:06,288
Every answer names its trace too, even an answer that says no.

18
00:01:06,338 --> 00:01:11,976
A merchant's server can quote that number to support, and support finds the whole story in seconds.

19
00:01:12,026 --> 00:01:14,592
Every log line carries the same number.

20
00:01:14,642 --> 00:01:21,264
So a warning in one log leads straight to the request that caused it, and to every other line about that request.

21
00:01:21,314 --> 00:01:23,136
Now the card numbers.

22
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:25,944
Before any line is written, it is cleaned.

23
00:01:25,994 --> 00:01:27,456
This is log masking.

24
00:01:27,506 --> 00:01:33,192
A card number keeps only its first six and its last four digits; the middle becomes stars.

25
00:01:33,242 --> 00:01:37,512
Any long run of digits that could be a card number is treated as one.

26
00:01:37,562 --> 00:01:40,656
A token keeps only its last four characters.

27
00:01:40,706 --> 00:01:43,632
Passwords and secrets are hidden completely.

28
00:01:43,682 --> 00:01:47,112
This happens on every line, wherever the line is sent.

29
00:01:47,162 --> 00:01:50,256
So nothing the running system writes can leak a card.

30
00:01:50,306 --> 00:01:53,424
Each product sends all of this to its own monitoring.

31
00:01:53,474 --> 00:01:57,456
ecom has its own, and the card zone runs its own, separately.

32
00:01:57,506 --> 00:02:00,144
They share nothing here either, just as on the map.

33
00:02:00,194 --> 00:02:02,016
Let's watch the product.

34
00:02:02,066 --> 00:02:05,208
A support person opens ecom's monitoring.

35
00:02:05,258 --> 00:02:10,584
The first dashboard shows payments by how they end: paid, declined, and unknown.

36
00:02:10,634 --> 00:02:15,792
Unknown is painted red: payments where nobody yet knows whether money moved.

37
00:02:15,842 --> 00:02:18,792
Right now, the red line sits at zero.

38
00:02:18,842 --> 00:02:21,432
They paste the trace number the merchant quoted.

39
00:02:21,482 --> 00:02:27,984
One line appears, crossing ecom, the card zone and the adapter, with the time each step took.

40
00:02:28,034 --> 00:02:30,240
They open the log lines of that request.

41
00:02:30,290 --> 00:02:35,352
The card number shows its first six digits, a row of stars, and its last four.

42
00:02:35,402 --> 00:02:38,448
Now the processing centre goes quiet for a while.

43
00:02:38,498 --> 00:02:42,216
Payments start to stay unknown, and the red line climbs.

44
00:02:42,266 --> 00:02:44,352
Nobody is watching the screen.

45
00:02:44,402 --> 00:02:46,968
That does not matter, because of alert rules.

46
00:02:47,018 --> 00:02:53,808
An alert rule is a condition the system checks on its own, all the time, written down like the rest of the product.

47
00:02:53,858 --> 00:02:57,984
When payments stay unknown for too long, the rule fires.

48
00:02:58,034 --> 00:03:00,624
An email arrives: payments are stuck.

49
00:03:00,674 --> 00:03:02,952
It says what is wrong and when it started.

50
00:03:03,002 --> 00:03:07,248
Support opens the dashboard from the email and sees the same red line.

51
00:03:07,298 --> 00:03:09,120
What does this protect?

52
00:03:09,170 --> 00:03:14,256
Without the trace, a merchant's simple question costs an hour of guessing.

53
00:03:14,306 --> 00:03:20,688
Without masking, the logs become a second place where card numbers live, and the locked room has a hole in its wall.

54
00:03:20,738 --> 00:03:25,296
Without alerts, a stuck payment is found by an angry buyer, not by the bank.

55
00:03:25,346 --> 00:03:27,600
The trace protects the merchant's time.

56
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:29,736
Masking protects the buyer's card.

57
00:03:29,786 --> 00:03:33,312
Alerts make sure the bank is the first to know, not the last.

58
00:03:33,362 --> 00:03:35,184
Back to the hospital.

59
00:03:35,234 --> 00:03:40,800
One number on every chart, in every department, and no secrets written on the wristband.

60
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:43,320
Next: what the merchant gets each day.
